1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:16,320
This is the Real AI and Digital Transformation Podcast Series, empowering technology and business professionals to succeed with AI and digital transformation.

2
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:20,480
Now, here's your host, best-selling author Thomas Earl.

3
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:26,960
Hi, and welcome to another episode of the Real AI and Digital Transformation Podcast Series.

4
00:00:27,246 --> 00:00:33,348
This is Thomas Earle, and my guest today is Aaron Bach, CTO and co-founder of Liminal.

5
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:34,450
Welcome, Aaron.

6
00:00:35,086 --> 00:00:35,760
Thank you, Thomas.

7
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:36,720
Great to be with you.

8
00:00:37,134 --> 00:00:37,440
Super.

9
00:00:37,520 --> 00:01:15,840
So, our topic today is static versus dynamic workflows, and the influence AI is having on how we reenvision the design and planning of our operational workflows, and how we're transitioning to a more dynamic model based on AI reasoning, capabilities, AI planning capabilities, and how that is impacting, not just the way we operate, but the way we need to build systems in order to take advantage of that dynamic capability.

10
00:01:16,974 --> 00:01:20,880
So based on your experience in that field, Aaron, where should we begin?

11
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:28,516
Where would you like to start that discussion to establish, that viewpoint of how things are transitioning?

12
00:01:29,358 --> 00:01:30,400
Yeah, certainly, Thomas.

13
00:01:31,374 --> 00:01:35,440
I have the opportunity to work in a lot of regulated industries.

14
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:44,720
So banking, financial services, healthcare, education, state-local government, industries, for lack of a better word, that have been around forever.

15
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:48,932
They're kind of central and critical to maybe all human endeavors here.

16
00:01:49,294 --> 00:02:05,440
And when I think about workflows in these industries, they're often this amalgam of, manual steps, endless data sources, processes that were set in stone, decades and decades ago, that maybe get appended to every now and then.

17
00:02:05,646 --> 00:02:17,360
But you can kind of almost envision that these industries have been operating under the highest scrutiny, the highest levels of need for whoever their quote unquote customers are, patients, students, et cetera.

18
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:33,040
And so what you end up having are processes and workflows that are built really around long-running changes, which is always, I think, a difficult thing for workloads to respond to, especially when they've traditionally been built in a very static way.

19
00:02:33,390 --> 00:02:48,080
But there's also some crust or some you know grime around them in terms of these industries have often faced some of the most strict penalties for breaking the regulatory compliance requirements that they're under.

20
00:02:48,398 --> 00:03:01,040
And so as times change, as technologies change, as the needs of their users change, I think what we find a lot of times are endless amounts of data, endless amounts of processes, endless amounts of owners.

21
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:06,400
And in the current age, which just as much as I know, you know, it's moving faster than ever.

22
00:03:06,702 --> 00:03:24,960
I think we see this interesting dynamic where there are multiple maybe factors at play that are, you know, on the negative side, preventing workflows from advancing in these industries, but also I think a handful of really tactical, capable steps that these industries can take to go in a more modern direction.

23
00:03:25,134 --> 00:03:26,720
So just an interesting time and moment.

24
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:36,880
And I always like to highlight these industries because, amidst the many, many industries under the planet, these are the ones that have been around forever, that we kind of assume we know what they do.

25
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:46,800
We know what education is, we know what healthcare is, and yet, you know, they kind of simultaneously stand to gain the most from maybe what AI has to offer in terms of how they operate.

26
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:56,320
they're also facing some pretty significant challenges in terms of shaking off some of the rust that they've built up over decades and decades and decades of a pre AI era.

27
00:03:58,542 --> 00:03:59,008
Right.

28
00:03:59,168 --> 00:04:13,728
It's so volatile right now in this regard, organizations all wanting to um take advantage of this new workflow design model, for lack of a better term.

29
00:04:14,094 --> 00:04:35,888
But in terms of how it actually is happening in the real world, when organizations explore this transition toward from static design, where everything is predetermined, they predict potential outcomes and conditions and then carry out the workflow logic.

30
00:04:35,918 --> 00:05:11,684
correspondingly, transitioning from that to a model that relies on the AI agents' capabilities to reason and figure out at runtime, what the best actions should be, and to then take action, and to then also determine where human oversight is still required to ensure that certain actions that may be improper or incorrect or may cause damage are are not taken, or at least verified before they are taken.

31
00:05:12,878 --> 00:05:21,088
what have you seen in terms of the actual activity in the trenches regarding the implementation of these systems?

32
00:05:21,328 --> 00:05:30,788
What type of challenges or issues or best practices have come out of the work in that regard so far?

33
00:05:31,808 --> 00:05:32,448
Certainly.

34
00:05:32,608 --> 00:05:42,288
You know, when I think about kind of the challenges I see, Thomas, I really see maybe a fence and then two, you know, gulfs, two cliffs on either side of that fence.

35
00:05:42,368 --> 00:05:46,276
And a lot of organizations are kind of heading towards one cliff or the other.

36
00:05:46,608 --> 00:05:57,008
You know, on one end, and I think this is the end that is very predominant within these regulated industries, there is a heavy sense of we need to go very slowly.

37
00:05:57,168 --> 00:05:59,524
We need to really understand every detail.

38
00:05:59,968 --> 00:06:03,168
we need to bake, huge process documentation.

39
00:06:03,248 --> 00:06:12,688
We need to understand and have focus groups around what we're even thinking about doing with regards to AI generated or AI-empowered revolutions in our industries.

40
00:06:12,848 --> 00:06:15,408
We need to have committees and we need to have those committees vote.

41
00:06:15,488 --> 00:06:17,508
And before you know it, 12 months have passed.

42
00:06:17,728 --> 00:06:22,288
You've got a lot of documentation, but very little reality to show for it.

43
00:06:22,368 --> 00:06:33,568
I think that's the first gulf is almost analysis, paralysis of attempting, you know, very understandably so, to look at this space of AI and think this is moving so unbelievably fast.

44
00:06:33,968 --> 00:06:36,448
we cannot allow it to blow us around.

45
00:06:36,528 --> 00:06:39,808
We need to kind of stay focused and oriented around what we do.

46
00:06:40,078 --> 00:06:41,808
It will adhere to our time frames.

47
00:06:42,048 --> 00:06:43,204
That's maybe one side.

48
00:06:43,694 --> 00:06:46,128
The other side, and you know, be surprised.

49
00:06:46,208 --> 00:07:03,888
I've seen plenty of organizations in regulated industries or regulated adjacent industries that are kind of going off the fence into the other gulf, which is much more, you know what, we trust our users and we're gonna put the tools in their hands and we're gonna see what the users bring forth because you know, grassroots initiatives are how real change occur, right?

50
00:07:03,968 --> 00:07:08,448
So we want to put the tools in their hands and let's see all the revolutions to come.

51
00:07:09,070 --> 00:07:19,568
And of course, you know, the danger on that side that we see really frequently is you watch often very well-intentioned users doing things with data or doing things with information that are incorrect.

52
00:07:19,662 --> 00:07:21,888
Um, that they put sensitive data at risk.

53
00:07:22,048 --> 00:07:29,348
They're drawing conclusions based on data that looks very correct, but a closer inspection turns out to be false.

54
00:07:29,728 --> 00:07:38,288
And so then there's the other side of the spectrum, people just racing forward, and we're going to keep up with the speed and we're going to meet the speed and we'll deal with the issues as they come.

55
00:07:39,022 --> 00:07:48,880
Obviously, I think both of those represent maybe the ultimate challenge of this time, Thomas, and that both of those, I don't think are ultimately going to serve these industries.

56
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:59,236
The former is going to reach the stage of, you know, as things move faster and faster, and people expect more and more, and there's more and more high-stakes conclusions that need to be drawn.

57
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:06,532
This incredibly manual, process-driven, you know, committee-backed approach is just fundamentally too slow.

58
00:08:07,150 --> 00:08:13,280
On the other hand, kind of just the Wild West approach of just putting it in, you know, the hands of the people on the front lines.

59
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:27,680
although it sounds wonderful, without the appropriate protections in place, you kind of have this notion that all of a sudden, all of the things that have kept chief information security officers up at night for decades and decades are accelerated.

60
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:33,280
People are doing things that who knows what they're doing, who knows what data they're accessing, who knows what tools they're using.

61
00:08:33,582 --> 00:08:40,160
By the way, AI has gone from zero to a trillion in an incredibly short span of time here.

62
00:08:40,366 --> 00:08:42,720
Who knows where that data is flying now?

63
00:08:43,022 --> 00:08:44,640
But it's now the cat's out of the bag.

64
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:47,200
And so we need to allow people to continue down that road.

65
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:50,080
the danger be damned in some ways.

66
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:52,480
And both of those have their risks, right?

67
00:08:52,560 --> 00:09:04,960
The former may keep you where you are and stuck in stasis, and the latter may put you at real risk of doing something inappropriate or harmful to the organization, its customers, its reputation.

68
00:09:05,230 --> 00:09:16,000
So when I think about kind of where have I seen organizations straddle the line here where they recognize AI is moving fast, it needs to revolutionize something in our business.

69
00:09:16,160 --> 00:09:24,036
We can't just go crazy here, but we also can't just plan and plan and plan and plan until we feel comfortable, because frankly, nobody feels comfortable right now.

70
00:09:24,430 --> 00:09:24,880
What do we do?

71
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:27,300
Well, I think there's a couple things that come to mind.

72
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:38,160
number one, promotion of AI literacy across the individuals who are going to be responsible for designing these workflows is so, so crucial.

73
00:09:38,414 --> 00:09:45,360
Um, you know, that that kind of contrarian view to the question of why is workflow design changing or even dying in an AI era.

74
00:09:45,934 --> 00:09:54,640
Well, on the one hand, building processes, building workflows, building operational playbooks, whatever it is, the rules are changing.

75
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:56,480
the speed at which it's done is changing.

76
00:09:56,560 --> 00:10:10,160
And if you've been doing this your entire career before AI, and you kind of just believe that AI will just do what you already do and accelerate it, you're going to be missing a whole host of nuance that at best is going to prevent you from experiencing.

77
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:15,120
And to your point, the the capabilities of these models and the associated ecosystems around them.

78
00:10:15,342 --> 00:10:24,960
But at worst, you may actually design something that was really appropriate for a bygone era, either structurally or philosophically, but it's just not going to work in this era.

79
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:36,560
So AI literacy, number one, is so crucial for the individuals to kind of update their core skills into an environment that is going to be fundamental to how we do work going forward.

80
00:10:36,910 --> 00:10:40,960
At the same time, I think traditional workflow design is dying for a second reason.

81
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:44,324
And that's because, here again, AI literacy is so crucial.

82
00:10:44,910 --> 00:10:49,540
People need to retain a sense of what they bring to the table as the human in the loop.

83
00:10:49,870 --> 00:11:00,560
I think right now, so much of the conversation is around look at all these agents, and the agents are talking to agents and they're making autonomous decisions, and we're seeing productivity go through the roof and all these things.

84
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:26,080
As somebody in these trenches myself, both with our customers, with my team, with the organizations we lead, I cannot tell you the number of times that the latest and greatest reasoning models of the Opus family or the GPT Soul family or whoever it is, they are really quick to arrive at a wonderful sounding conclusion that very often, upon further prodding, has a lot of kind of problematic holes in it.

85
00:11:26,286 --> 00:11:33,440
Or at the very least, it hasn't really thought through a lot of the nuance that you or I, the experts at this craft, know to think about.

86
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:37,760
And so this AI literacy component, it's not very exciting.

87
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:42,160
it's not sort of rocket science that anybody was going to write home to grandma about.

88
00:11:42,830 --> 00:11:55,680
But I think the idea of, if you're going to have to balance between these two goals, then you also need to balance a sense of bringing yourself into an environment where your skills need to be updated to understand: how do models work, how do agents work?

89
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:57,120
How do their ecosystems work?

90
00:11:57,280 --> 00:12:11,812
When is one right for the right purpose or wrong for the wrong purpose, while at the same time recognizing that the very best organizations are orchestrating these generative AI, agentic AI workflows in such a manner they still have to pass the sniff test?

91
00:12:12,080 --> 00:12:19,760
So to speak, of the individuals who are not only responsible for, but who are knowledgeable of the organization and what it's trying to do.

92
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:25,440
So that kind of number one AI literacy is, I think, such a crucial place that you have to start at.

93
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:27,600
You can't, you can't stay out of the game.

94
00:12:27,854 --> 00:12:29,920
You can't just let people run off into their own game.

95
00:12:30,080 --> 00:12:34,500
The organization's got to be the one to say, we are gonna become AI literate, and here's how that's going to be done.

96
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:36,400
Right.

97
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:42,240
And the so you you, you talked about how, uh, you know, the based on your experience.

98
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:49,040
the agents can be making decisions that, um, are clearly not correct.

99
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:52,880
and perhaps that's part of their evolutionary process.

100
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:59,760
once they are initially implemented and over time, that may be something that is improved.

101
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:10,320
But in terms of you know, incorporating human in the loop as part of the overall solution design, what have you seen?

102
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:13,920
Like to what extent is that necessary early on?

103
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:29,124
And then have you seen it being scaled back over time as more trust and confidence is had in the AI's capabilities to reason and make decisions?

104
00:13:29,998 --> 00:13:38,480
And perhaps is it related to the nature of decisions, whether they're low-risk decisions versus high-stakes decisions, and so on.

105
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:39,588
Where have you?

106
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:40,320
um?

107
00:13:41,230 --> 00:13:48,560
how have you seen that transition over time in terms of an organization that sets it up?

108
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:49,156
initially?

109
00:13:49,360 --> 00:13:58,640
uh, starts putting operational responsibility in this new AI-based solution with human oversight?

110
00:13:58,720 --> 00:14:01,860
And then over time, how have you seen that evolve?

111
00:14:02,720 --> 00:14:03,520
Great question.

112
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:17,120
one of the things I've seen, Thomas, that I really do align with is sort of the notion of the centralization of the program, but the democratization or sort of the downward allocation of the execution.

113
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:28,160
One of the ways I kind of think about it, you know, I think back to the 1960s, you know, when the space program in the United States was kind of getting going, there was this belief that, you know.

114
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:45,440
the government itself was in the best position to design the safety standards, the requirements, sort of the long-term programming of what manned spaceflight would look like, and kind of accelerate into this timeframe where you have a bunch of, you know, private entities that are embarking on something that traditionally had been the purview of the government.

115
00:14:45,600 --> 00:15:06,400
I think you'd hear a lot of scientists and a lot of experts in the arena say that there's an interesting model there of having sort of a governing body create the framework, but then allowing or democratizing appropriate entities, teams, groups, whatever that is downstream to actually draw in the colors, so to speak, to fill in that playbook with actual execution.

116
00:15:06,670 --> 00:15:09,600
I actually think that applies here really, really well.

117
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:12,480
Um, you know, and maybe to kind of give some analogies.

118
00:15:12,560 --> 00:15:16,800
Obviously, I, you know, I'm being a CTO, I'm drenched in software development all day.

119
00:15:17,134 --> 00:15:33,920
Maybe one of the ways I'll look at this is what do we, how do how we approach that problem as a software development community, my team, other CTOs that I know, and we'll draw some parallels there between what is a very technical craft, but also some general principles that I think apply regardless of what your craft is.

120
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:38,756
That, you know, these same principles will apply if you're an accountant or a lawyer or some other frontline worker.

121
00:15:39,214 --> 00:15:55,748
Um, you know, in our case, when we started accelerating our efforts, and I'll say here our efforts that you can fill in the blank for whatever role, one of the things that we knew right away was that we needed appropriate kind of structural guardrails around what was correct in our code base.

122
00:15:56,014 --> 00:16:03,200
So, you know, before we ever approached anything with regard to AI, we wanted a lot of tests that would test, you know, the code that we'd written.

123
00:16:03,374 --> 00:16:12,000
We wanted a lot of standards in place for how to format the code, how to structure the code, and where it should, you know, operate independently of those standards.

124
00:16:12,366 --> 00:16:17,060
We were fortunate in that all of those standards had kind of been part of our craft for a long time.

125
00:16:17,550 --> 00:16:24,132
But because they were documented in our code base, every time we submit a piece of code, it's got to check these seven things.

126
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:28,640
Right off the bat, the AI had something to work against.

127
00:16:29,198 --> 00:16:40,320
meaning when we started augmenting our software development with AI generated code, right off the bat, it knew if it just did something that broke one of our tests, that that was considered a failure.

128
00:16:40,462 --> 00:16:42,880
And it needed to go back and understand why it broke the test.

129
00:16:42,990 --> 00:16:48,640
And more often than not, that breakage indicated a problem in its logic, not a problem in the test itself.

130
00:16:49,294 --> 00:16:56,784
Similarly, you know, the large language models at the time were all over the place in terms of how they formatted code, structured code, et cetera.

131
00:16:57,024 --> 00:17:11,504
Because they could read our standards, and because we had some tooling in place to reject code that didn't meet our standards, the AI really kind of ran into a brick wall if it at the bare minimum didn't adhere just, you know, seven or eight of these core principles.

132
00:17:11,664 --> 00:17:14,544
And so I'll pause right there and think about any industry.

133
00:17:14,926 --> 00:17:19,184
Being able to identify for your particular function what are the core principles?

134
00:17:19,344 --> 00:17:21,540
What are we not willing to flex on?

135
00:17:21,934 --> 00:17:29,264
And how can those be represented in code, in workflow, in the actual running of the piece of software?

136
00:17:29,646 --> 00:17:46,224
One of the benefits is that if you can kind of very clearly define what those guardrails are and you can make your code, your workflow, your operation fail when those are not held, the AI gets a sense of, oh, I just tried to do something and the something caused all these failures.

137
00:17:46,318 --> 00:17:48,292
Let me go read what those failures are.

138
00:17:48,718 --> 00:17:55,744
It was one of those moments, Thomas, where we kind of realized early on, you know, maybe that maybe the models will become smart enough someday to not need all this guidance.

139
00:17:56,014 --> 00:18:01,092
But given how fast they operate, I would rather they run into brick walls 17 times.

140
00:18:01,422 --> 00:18:11,012
If the ultimate outcome is at the end, they didn't necessarily quote unquote learn anything about us, but they understood hitting all these guardrails required them to get to a correct outcome.

141
00:18:11,264 --> 00:18:20,704
Now I could tell you a much longer story there, but that's kind of the road we've been having is more and more guardrails in place that in actual execution will fail if they're not met.

142
00:18:21,070 --> 00:18:27,584
And the AI is now understanding, man, I may not know this thing Aaron's asking me to do, but I know I just got a big red false right here.

143
00:18:27,664 --> 00:18:29,264
So I better fix that.

144
00:18:29,582 --> 00:18:41,476
The more of those types of guardrails we've had in place, the more I think stage two has begun, in which it's been possible for us to be more ambitious of the problems that we set AI against.

145
00:18:41,902 --> 00:18:53,636
We early on set it against simple little refactors, simple little tiny features, little bug fixes, the little things that were, to your point, important to us and our customers, but it wasn't going to burn the world down if it failed.

146
00:18:54,030 --> 00:18:59,664
And in a way, the AI needed to show us that it was going to listen to our instructions before we turned it loose on heavier things.

147
00:18:59,744 --> 00:19:04,868
And we've now gotten to a point where we've turned it loose on heavy amounts of things and it's holding to those guardrails.

148
00:19:05,358 --> 00:19:21,092
All that to say, and I'll wrap this point here, even with all that, there are a series of kind of playbooks that we have that kind of will cause us to go, even if the AI thinks this thing is done or it's really confident in it.

149
00:19:21,870 --> 00:19:27,184
the team we kind of use an emoji-based standard, and uh one of our emojis that will give each other is the eyeballs emoji.

150
00:19:27,264 --> 00:19:39,344
And that means this AI thinks it's done everything correctly here, but this A is either crucial enough, it's large scale enough, B, it's giving my spider sense some tingling.

151
00:19:39,534 --> 00:19:42,384
I think something is off here, or really any other reason.

152
00:19:42,544 --> 00:19:44,624
I want another human being to review this.

153
00:19:44,784 --> 00:19:48,324
I want you to look at what's happened here, raise your own concerns.

154
00:19:49,422 --> 00:19:52,224
and some of that people ask, well, how do you how do you put that in code?

155
00:19:52,304 --> 00:20:10,544
How do you encapsulate Aaron's 20 plus years of knowing that that's where Aaron, his team, other in your various craft, that's where the human in the loop is so important because I don't know that I can tell you exactly moments in time where I could have an algorithm, an algorithmically based approach to understanding why a piece of code was problematic.

156
00:20:10,830 --> 00:20:13,824
I just knew again, it struck my spider sense.

157
00:20:13,904 --> 00:20:22,464
And that's where when I think about this process, regardless of function, I've talked a lot about software development here, but regardless of function, there's a lot of ebb and flow.

158
00:20:22,638 --> 00:20:26,144
There's put some guardrails in place, try a couple tasks, watch where it fails.

159
00:20:26,414 --> 00:20:29,424
Improve those guardrails, try a couple tasks, watch where it fails.

160
00:20:29,584 --> 00:20:36,624
At some point, reach a confidence threshold where you up the threshold on what kind of task you're going to give it.

161
00:20:36,942 --> 00:20:40,932
Maybe it's something more difficult, maybe something more critical, maybe something that's time sensitive.

162
00:20:41,422 --> 00:21:00,944
Pressure test it, see where the guardrails, guardrails fail, keep going, all the while training yourself to know, man, I in my particular craft, software development and accounting, in legal, in frontline healthcare, office administration, whatever it is, don't lose a sense of where's my spider sense in all this?

163
00:21:01,104 --> 00:21:04,224
The things that previously I just knew because I was in them all the time.

164
00:21:04,462 --> 00:21:14,020
How can I continue to apply that sense of what is correct or what might be problematic and just be willing to slow the AI down when that spider sense tingles?

165
00:21:14,848 --> 00:21:17,888
That kind of sequence there, Thomas, is what I've seen a lot of success with.

166
00:21:18,222 --> 00:21:23,888
One of the statements I've used before, I use a lot, it's an old adage, slow is smooth and smooth is fast.

167
00:21:23,968 --> 00:21:27,808
And everybody wants to race forward and do all these huge initiatives with AI.

168
00:21:28,078 --> 00:21:36,196
Really getting comfortable to where the operation, even a micro operation, is smooth, that's the foundation from which speed is drawn.

169
00:21:36,528 --> 00:21:42,688
I think the organizations I've seen really struggle and sometimes fail are the ones who say we need to revolutionize the business overnight.

170
00:21:42,798 --> 00:21:45,168
And speed ultimately leads to a downfall there.

171
00:21:45,248 --> 00:21:50,180
So obviously we could talk a lot more there, but hopefully that gives you a sense of kind of some sequencing that I've seen work.

172
00:21:51,246 --> 00:21:53,648
Yeah, that's very insightful.

173
00:21:53,728 --> 00:22:07,812
And I guess just before we conclude, one last question in relation to that specifically in terms of the overall speed of execution of a reasoning-based workflow.

174
00:22:08,128 --> 00:22:47,008
Um, we've talked about failure conditions and human oversight, but how have you seen organizations measure the efficiency of these dynamic workflows to ensure that they are providing enough improvement in terms of how quickly, how responsibly, and how error-free they may be carrying out operations to justify the whole transition toward this type of workflow model?

175
00:22:47,088 --> 00:22:57,604
How are organizations tracking that to get them the statistics, the metrics necessary for them to evaluate it?

176
00:22:58,368 --> 00:22:59,408
It's a great question.

177
00:22:59,728 --> 00:23:00,848
I'll be candid with you.

178
00:23:00,928 --> 00:23:03,492
I've seen a lot of organizations struggle to do this.

179
00:23:04,048 --> 00:23:06,048
I'll speak to somebody else on the front lines.

180
00:23:06,288 --> 00:23:08,768
I've struggled at times to know exactly how to do this.

181
00:23:08,848 --> 00:23:20,484
And the the struggle, Thomas, is not based on capability, but I think it's based on the propensity for these metrics to be sort of innovation theater or vanity metrics.

182
00:23:21,008 --> 00:23:29,488
in a software development case, it is really easy to talk about how many changes we made to the code base, how many features we added, how many bugs we fixed.

183
00:23:29,742 --> 00:23:43,008
Those aren't necessarily bad things, but 20 years ago, at the very beginning of my career, all the rage, maybe from the decade prior, had been we want to see our software developers writing, as much code as possible.

184
00:23:43,088 --> 00:23:50,208
And we're going to incentivize them around, you know, the uh kind of compensation on the number of thousands of lines of code that they've written.

185
00:23:50,478 --> 00:23:57,796
And of course, a lot of maybe unscrupulous software engineers learned, well, I can write a lot of really bad code and still meet my incentive structure.

186
00:23:58,190 --> 00:24:13,408
So when I say I think I see organizations struggle, I think they often start with a very understandable thing, which is we need to measure our agents and how many tasks are they executing, how many of those tasks were successful versus a failure state, how many of those failure states were corrected with subsequent actions.

187
00:24:13,678 --> 00:24:15,396
That's all good stuff.

188
00:24:15,728 --> 00:24:23,808
It really runs the risk of being kind of metric theater unless you can directly tie it to an outcome that matters to your organization.

189
00:24:23,968 --> 00:24:25,220
We know what these are.

190
00:24:25,488 --> 00:24:34,372
It's increase in revenue, it's patient retention, it's a reduction in churn of our customer base, it's the graduation rate of our students.

191
00:24:34,958 --> 00:24:51,648
At the end of the day, when I see this idea kind of percolate that AI is going to accelerate us as an organization, I'm stunned, Thomas, how regularly there's a disconnection between the rapidity of deployment of these solutions from the fundamental thing.

192
00:24:51,728 --> 00:24:52,932
We need to graduate our students.

193
00:24:53,294 --> 00:24:57,408
We need to provide better patient outcomes, we need to increase our organization's revenue, right?

194
00:24:57,488 --> 00:25:00,288
All the, again, not very sexy stuff, but so important.

195
00:25:00,654 --> 00:25:04,768
And instead, we focus on all these sort of, metrics around what our agents are doing.

196
00:25:04,928 --> 00:25:10,372
So, number one, I think there needs to be an agreed upon success metric that includes.

197
00:25:10,960 --> 00:25:18,960
Technology, whether that's a CTO, the CIO, the CISO, whoever that is, there needs to be a success metric that everybody, including technology, can agree on.

198
00:25:19,214 --> 00:25:25,600
And that success metric has to be tied to the human outcome of our users, our customers, whatever they are.

199
00:25:25,840 --> 00:25:31,600
From there, I think you're in a wonderful place to start measuring the efficacy of your AI workflows.

200
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:36,960
And that's where I do think you can start to look at things like, how quickly are they responding to issues?

201
00:25:37,230 --> 00:25:42,880
How many times do they have to re-jigger themselves to get to a successful outcome when they hit an error on the first retry?

202
00:25:43,054 --> 00:25:48,480
How many times did they express uncertainty at a particular step in the workflow?

203
00:25:48,782 --> 00:25:53,680
That can all be interesting data that can help you tune and tweak the pipeline.

204
00:25:54,222 --> 00:25:58,080
But fundamentally, the engine that you're building is going to run a car.

205
00:25:58,286 --> 00:25:59,680
You need to know where that car is going.

206
00:25:59,760 --> 00:26:01,120
You need to know why you're going there.

207
00:26:01,200 --> 00:26:05,860
You need to ensure that what engine you're building is going to get there along the lines that matter to you most.

208
00:26:06,480 --> 00:26:10,800
And again, without that, I think there runs a risk of metrics being metric theater.

209
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:16,644
But again, every now and then, I'll see a, especially in these regulated industries, I'll see an organization that has a clear sense.

210
00:26:17,520 --> 00:26:20,720
one in particular, I'll just share kind of off the cuff here.

211
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:23,120
this organization is healthcare adjacent.

212
00:26:23,278 --> 00:26:25,476
They're sort of in the medical benefits space.

213
00:26:25,760 --> 00:26:30,160
Maybe not an industry that you would think would be terribly hooked to innovation in general.

214
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:39,360
These folks have a great sense of who their customer base is, what drives the needle for their customer base, which is, you know, in this interesting inflection point as um.

215
00:26:39,360 --> 00:26:50,720
they're starting to transition kind of maybe into that baby boomer era where that is the bulk of their users, but they see a day coming where millennials and Gen Z and Gen Alpha and all that are going to be the predominant users.

216
00:26:50,990 --> 00:26:57,600
They're starting to kind of really understand what matters to these individuals for the ultimate goal of we want people to stay with us.

217
00:26:57,680 --> 00:27:01,680
We want them to be so delighted by their benefits, they don't think of looking anywhere else.

218
00:27:01,934 --> 00:27:14,000
From that place, they've built workflows around, you know, near-term, assessing feedback data and understanding where their benefits programs or the mechanisms that deliver those programs are falling down with their current customers.

219
00:27:14,480 --> 00:27:18,080
they've developed workflows that allow their executives to kind of plan.

220
00:27:18,160 --> 00:27:23,680
If we were to totally revolutionize the medical benefits space without any constraints, what might we do?

221
00:27:24,014 --> 00:27:27,200
You know, something that traditionally would take a huge consulting engagement.

222
00:27:27,280 --> 00:27:28,640
Now they can do pretty rapidly.

223
00:27:28,846 --> 00:27:37,200
But it's all based around this idea of we want to create exemplary outcomes for our members such that they don't ever consider leaving us for another benefits provider.

224
00:27:37,486 --> 00:27:39,040
And that's been really neat to watch, Thomas.

225
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:48,240
And, again, I didn't come into this thinking that medical benefits space was particularly tied to innovation, but this one organization that we're fortunate to work with, man, they have figured out what matters to them.

226
00:27:48,494 --> 00:27:56,960
They understand the landscape and they are deploying these operations, these agents, these workflows in a targeted way, very clearly connected to that ultimate outcome.

227
00:27:57,230 --> 00:28:04,080
And then on top of that, they can start to measure well, every time we do a strategy session where we think outside the bounds, how many of those ideas are actually being implemented?

228
00:28:04,398 --> 00:28:05,440
How many have actually gone stale?

229
00:28:05,520 --> 00:28:07,920
How many were actually shot down by subsequent data?

230
00:28:08,238 --> 00:28:13,120
All of that is beginning to teach them more and more kind of alignment towards their ultimate goal.

231
00:28:13,280 --> 00:28:14,884
So it definitely is happening.

232
00:28:15,040 --> 00:28:20,960
Um, and there is that mixture of you got to get people in the game experimenting with this stuff to gain that AI literacy.

233
00:28:21,262 --> 00:28:23,440
You got to do it in a way that's safe and secure, obviously.

234
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:34,852
Um, it definitely can be done, but it in a gosh, in a rapidly moving space, it requires some very strong dedication and diligence to just stay on that fence between those two gulfs I mentioned earlier.

235
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:36,400
Super.

236
00:28:36,640 --> 00:28:38,480
Wow, lots to think about.

237
00:28:38,960 --> 00:28:44,000
Aaron, before we conclude, tell us a bit more about what you're doing at Liminal.

238
00:28:45,056 --> 00:28:45,456
Absolutely.

239
00:28:45,856 --> 00:29:03,376
Liminal was founded, Thomas, out of the idea that and I mentioned these regulated industries, that in many ways they stood to gain the most potentially from AI in terms of all we've talked about here, renovating processes, accelerating their capabilities, kind of bringing their operations into the main line of the 21st century.

240
00:29:03,630 --> 00:29:10,096
But at the same time, you know, we had a lot of experience with financial services and others who they're terrified of AI.

241
00:29:10,176 --> 00:29:11,856
They're terrified of losing data.

242
00:29:12,016 --> 00:29:13,776
They're terrified of information leaking.

243
00:29:13,856 --> 00:29:17,856
They're terrified of people making poor decisions based on generated information.

244
00:29:18,016 --> 00:29:24,176
And Limo was really born out of the idea that you could serve both kind of goals at the end of the day.

245
00:29:24,366 --> 00:29:41,616
You could serve a user who really wanted to use AI to the full breadth of its capabilities for their specific job function while at the same time providing organizational control, observability, protection of sensitive data, and all the things that are necessary to convince an organization in these regulated spaces.

246
00:29:41,936 --> 00:29:50,656
I'm still compliant with all the regulatory compliance measures that I have to be compliant with, but I can finally unlock my user base to use the full power of AI.

247
00:29:50,736 --> 00:29:56,292
So, you know, liminal, its name right there just kind of means the space between spaces.

248
00:29:56,654 --> 00:30:03,216
Traditionally, it's been very difficult for technology organizations like cybersecurity to really get on board with something like AI.

249
00:30:03,376 --> 00:30:06,016
The users want it and they're kind of doing it their own way.

250
00:30:06,446 --> 00:30:12,164
Liminal really exists to kind of bridge the gulf between them and enable safe, secure, and compliant AI for these industries.

251
00:30:13,774 --> 00:30:14,176
Super.

252
00:30:15,214 --> 00:30:35,136
It'll be interesting to see how all of this continues to take shape over the next year or two, and what the common approach to workflow design will be in the years to come, based on the maturity of the technology and the maturity of the practice in implementing the technology.

253
00:30:35,296 --> 00:30:47,456
So I think that what you're dealing with right now will become more and more important as time goes on and will basically be of interest to any organization in any industry.

254
00:30:48,270 --> 00:30:50,176
it looks very, very broad.

255
00:30:50,656 --> 00:30:52,976
Aaron, thank you so much for your time today.

256
00:30:53,454 --> 00:30:54,496
Yeah, thank you so much, Thomas.

257
00:30:54,656 --> 00:30:55,216
Great conversation.

258
00:30:55,376 --> 00:30:56,708
Really appreciate getting to chat with you.

259
00:30:57,296 --> 00:30:58,336
Thank you for listening.

